## Resurrection now reported (2026-08-18)
Was a known gap: a record published as withdrawn that reappears at the source was
served again with nothing said, because the contract passes and the classifier has no
failure to report. In a recall feed a relisted recalled product is the most actionable
event there is, so silence was the wrong default.

  runCycle now computes resurrectedRefs from state.withdrawnRefs against the current
  extraction, drops them from withdrawnRefs (withdrawn is a current status, not a
  permanent mark), and opens an incident with cause "resurrected". Not a failure: no
  repair, no refusal, rows served verified. Two tests in runner.test.ts.

Observed for real earlier tonight: restoring the baseline fixture brought TW-88214,
TW-44903 and TW-33887 back and the cycle said nothing. That run is what prompted this.

## UI redesign delegated to Kimi, and an output verifier (2026-08-18)
Design pass by kimi-k3-max in an isolated worktree, reviewed then integrated.
The change that mattered: hue now means exactly one thing (what the system decided
about a record) and severity moved to a segmented bar, so a refusal and a serious
risk no longer share the same clay. Refusals promoted to the front page verbatim
under solid stamps. A <details> disclosure hiding contract breaches was removed.
Verified independently before integrating: build passes, no horizontal overflow at
390/768/1280/1920, one h1 per page, no seller rendering, quarantine visually
distinct with source trust moved off the row.

Added web/verify-output.mjs, run at the end of `npm run build`. Checks the exported
HTML against the snapshot: every engine-authored string verbatim, no seller field.
Mutation-tested both rules.

  First version was WRONG and reported success on a truncated refusal. It joined all
  pages into one blob, and the same refusal is quoted on the front page and in the
  incident log, so the intact copy satisfied the search for the mangled one. Now
  checked per page: any page containing the first 40 characters must contain the whole
  string. 51 strings, 59 renderings, and the truncation is caught and located.

## Audit pass, and three holes in the output verifier (2026-08-18)
Read-only audit by kimi-k3-high in an isolated worktree, briefed to attack
verify-output.mjs specifically. Its shell was blocked, so it could not build or
measure; it said so up front and marked every unverifiable claim rather than
asserting it. Two of the three verifier holes came from that run.

Holes, all three confirmed by mutation against a real build:
  v1  concatenated the pages, so a refusal intact on /incidents satisfied the
      check for the same refusal truncated on /. (found earlier, by my own
      mutation test)
  v2  held a page to a string only if the page carried its first 40 chars, so
      {i.refusal.slice(38)} on / dropped that page out of the checked set while
      /incidents satisfied it. Tail truncation caught, head truncation not.
  v3  stripped <script> tags but kept their contents. A static Next export
      inlines every server-rendered string in the RSC flight payload, so
      deleting the visible breaches block left them findable in the payload.
      Confirmed in the bytes: "row count fell from 14 to 11" appears in
      out/incidents.html at offset 8929 in the DOM and 29953 inside a script.

v4 names the pages each string must appear on, discounts nesting (a refusal
quotes its own breaches, so raw fragment scanning reported every nesting as a
truncation), strips script and style contents, and bans hiding constructs
outright rather than claiming to detect them. Six mutations now caught:
head truncation, tail truncation, payload-only rendering, whole-section
deletion, disclosure hiding, seller leak.

Measured in a real browser (playwright, chromium), which the audit could not do:
  no horizontal overflow at 390/768/1280/1920, three pages, both schemes
  exactly one h1 per page
  three theme states resolve correctly, unstamped follows prefers-color-scheme
    in both directions, explicit light beats a dark OS
  skip link moves focus to #main after tabIndex={-1} was added
  --rule-strong now clears 3:1 on card/paper/sunken in both themes
    light  3.89 / 3.51 / 3.19   (was 2.77 / 2.50 / 2.27)
    dark   3.59 / 3.89 / 3.25   (was 2.67 / 2.89 / 2.41)
  the audit's hand-computed contrast matched the browser to 2dp

